{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LjFxvFtpgCjKYgcEEdgoKK","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on the context filesystem, the KRaft timeout-metrics design discussion concluded that **fetch/election timeout count metrics should always report a value regardless of the node's current epoch state**, rather than being registered/unregistered depending on whether the corresponding timer exists.\\\\n\\\\nThis decision was reached through discussion between Tony Tang and Kevin Wu. Here's the key resolution:\\\\n\\\\nFrom **`kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-14-mail-discuss-21488e5b.md`** (lines 53-59):\\\\n\\\\nTony Tang acknowledged the issue with selective registration: \\\\\\\"if we only register the election timeout metric during Unattached/Prospective/Candidate, those are all short states. After a timeout the node changes status, and the metric gets unregistered. Since nodes spend most of their time in Follower or Leader, operators may never see this metric at all.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nKevin Wu had argued for this approach earlier in **`kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-13-mail-aa4a89deddc8b16336616ab2-24eac6ec.md`** (lines 23-43), but ultimately concluded that downstream observability platforms may not capture metric values well if they're registered/unregistered. He noted that since \\\\\\\"timer expirations specifically cause transitions to and between more 'intermittent' states (e.g. Prospective, Candidate, etc.), and therefore occur at the 'end' of an EpochState, so registering and unregistering them does not seem like a good idea.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n**Final Decision** (from **`kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-14-mail-discuss-21488e5b.md`**, lines 86-89):\\\\n> \\\\\\\"In summary: 1. we always report the metrics. 2. we only increment the counter in the poll methods when isExpired() is called, as we originally planned.\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"store\\\",\\\"kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-14-mail-discuss-21488e5b.md\\\",\\\"kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-13-mail-aa4a89deddc8b16336616ab2-24eac6ec.md\\\",\\\"kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-12-mail-e24330f96e5db037c1aa3178-7bb6529e.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":23.73,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":2,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":37912,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":27048,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.0659212,\\\"input_tokens\\\":34,\\\"output_tokens\\\":1600}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9be8f8a1-903e-4c2e-b632-f13e000f697c","uuid":"2b576a0c-ab58-441a-a1b8-338026cae3fe","timestamp":"2026-09-27T19:21:21.925Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on the context filesystem, the KRaft timeout-metrics design discussion concluded that **fetch/election timeout count metrics should always report a value regardless of the node's current epoch state**, rather than being registered/unregistered depending on whether the corresponding timer exists.\\\\n\\\\nThis decision was reached through discussion between Tony Tang and Kevin Wu. Here's the key resolution:\\\\n\\\\nFrom **`kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-14-mail-discuss-21488e5b.md`** (lines 53-59):\\\\n\\\\nTony Tang acknowledged the issue with selective registration: \\\\\\\"if we only register the election timeout metric during Unattached/Prospective/Candidate, those are all short states. After a timeout the node changes status, and the metric gets unregistered. Since nodes spend most of their time in Follower or Leader, operators may never see this metric at all.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nKevin Wu had argued for this approach earlier in **`kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-13-mail-aa4a89deddc8b16336616ab2-24eac6ec.md`** (lines 23-43), but ultimately concluded that downstream observability platforms may not capture metric values well if they're registered/unregistered. He noted that since \\\\\\\"timer expirations specifically cause transitions to and between more 'intermittent' states (e.g. Prospective, Candidate, etc.), and therefore occur at the 'end' of an EpochState, so registering and unregistering them does not seem like a good idea.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n**Final Decision** (from **`kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-14-mail-discuss-21488e5b.md`**, lines 86-89):\\\\n> \\\\\\\"In summary: 1. we always report the metrics. 2. we only increment the counter in the poll methods when isExpired() is called, as we originally planned.\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"store\\\",\\\"kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-14-mail-discuss-21488e5b.md\\\",\\\"kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-13-mail-aa4a89deddc8b16336616ab2-24eac6ec.md\\\",\\\"kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-12-mail-e24330f96e5db037c1aa3178-7bb6529e.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":23.73,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":2,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":37912,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":27048,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.0659212,\\\"input_tokens\\\":34,\\\"output_tokens\\\":1600}}\"}","_meta":{"fastmcp":{"wrap_result":true}},"structuredContent":{"result":"{\"answer\":\"Based on the context filesystem, the KRaft timeout-metrics design discussion concluded that **fetch/election timeout count metrics should always report a value regardless of the node's current epoch state**, rather than being registered/unregistered depending on whether the corresponding timer exists.\\n\\nThis decision was reached through discussion between Tony Tang and Kevin Wu. Here's the key resolution:\\n\\nFrom **`kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-14-mail-discuss-21488e5b.md`** (lines 53-59):\\n\\nTony Tang acknowledged the issue with selective registration: \\\"if we only register the election timeout metric during Unattached/Prospective/Candidate, those are all short states. After a timeout the node changes status, and the metric gets unregistered. Since nodes spend most of their time in Follower or Leader, operators may never see this metric at all.\\\"\\n\\nKevin Wu had argued for this approach earlier in **`kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-13-mail-aa4a89deddc8b16336616ab2-24eac6ec.md`** (lines 23-43), but ultimately concluded that downstream observability platforms may not capture metric values well if they're registered/unregistered. He noted that since \\\"timer expirations specifically cause transitions to and between more 'intermittent' states (e.g. Prospective, Candidate, etc.), and therefore occur at the 'end' of an EpochState, so registering and unregistering them does not seem like a good idea.\\\"\\n\\n**Final Decision** (from **`kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-14-mail-discuss-21488e5b.md`**, lines 86-89):\\n> \\\"In summary: 1. we always report the metrics. 2. we only increment the counter in the poll methods when isExpired() is called, as we originally planned.\\\"\",\"error\":null,\"files\":[\"store\",\"kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-14-mail-discuss-21488e5b.md\",\"kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-13-mail-aa4a89deddc8b16336616ab2-24eac6ec.md\",\"kips/KIP-1322/2026-05-12-mail-e24330f96e5db037c1aa3178-7bb6529e.md\"],\"seconds\":23.73,\"truncated\":false,\"turns\":2,\"usage\":{\"cache_read_tokens\":37912,\"cache_write_tokens\":27048,\"cost_usd\":0.0659212,\"input_tokens\":34,\"output_tokens\":1600}}"}}}
